{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793973968,"nonce":"5NTYY+4/MVNzKelVq+2piBx28R4=","title":"Win64 ash_static_call keeps its old rsp in volatile r11 across the callee","message":"Reported by RayDE (their git-bug 819fd7e, CI run 41520026431): the packaged Windows app crashes at startup with 0xC0000005 inside libhl!ash_static_call+0x26c with an invalid stack pointer.\n\nThe x86_64 Windows arm of ash_static_call (std/src/fun.rs) does \"mov r11, rsp; and rsp,-16; sub rsp,32; call r10; mov rsp, r11\". r11 is volatile under Win64, so any callee may overwrite it and the restore loads a garbage rsp. It survives when the callee happens not to touch r11 and fails when it does, which is why it depends on the build and host.\n\nFix: hold the old rsp in rsi, which Win64 makes the callee preserve, and declare it clobbered. A Windows-only unit test calls a naked function that overwrites r10/r11 through ash_static_call.\n\nNot verified locally: no Windows toolchain here, so the change and the test compile only in the Windows CI job. Arguments past the fourth are still dropped (2c09faa).","files":null}]}